SUMMARY:[現場] Eugene Wang: "Biocosmic Art\; or How to Capture a Painter
 ’s Brain?"
LOCATION:Lathrop Library\, 224
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/eugene-wang-biocosmic-art-or-how-to-c
 apture-a-painters-brain
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/eugene-wang-biocosmic-art-or-
 how-to-capture-a-painters-brain\n\nAbstract:\n\nWhen medical imaging mappe
 d the neural deterioration of the legendary Hong Kong ink painter Irene Ch
 ou (1924-2011)\, she looked at her own brain X-rays and experienced a star
 tling revelation: “I saw vital images of tree branches… that is life
 —my life.” In this lecture\, Eugene Wang (Harvard University) reconsid
 ers Chou’s art through the converging lenses of plant sensience\, system
 s biology\, and Buddhist-cum-Daoist meditative practice\, revealing her pa
 intings not as abstract landscapes but as experiments in biocosmic imagina
 tion. From the refuge of her father’s photographic darkroom in Shanghai 
 to her solitary\, obsessive sketches of “ten thousand trees” in Hong K
 ong’s dense woods\, Chou turned ink into a means of self-preservation: t
 housands of rhythmic parallel lines became protective membranes\, holding 
 an endangered inner world against chaos. Bringing Charles Darwin’s studi
 es of plant movement\, Herbert Read’s modernist aesthetics\, medical ima
 ging\, and the alchemical cosmology of breathing exercise into dialogue\, 
 Wang shows how Chou transformed illness and vulnerability into paintings o
 f biocosmic imaginative reach—works that move from capillary and cell to
  vortex and galaxy\, forging a radiant visual lexicon of resilience\, rene
 wal\, and cosmic liberation. \n\nBio:\n\nEugene Y. Wang is the Abby Aldric
 h Rockefeller Professor of Asian Art at Harvard University. A Guggenheim F
 ellow and art-history editor of the Encyclopedia of Buddhism\, he is the a
 uthor of Shaping the Lotus Su
END:VEVENT
